{"name":"Armadillo","hex":"#484a46","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"armadillo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the burrowing mammals in the families Dasypodidae, Chlamyphoridae, Tolypeutidae and Euphractidae in the order Cingulata, found in the Americas, especially South America, and covered with bony, jointed protective plates."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/armadillo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Armadillo","blocks":[{"text":"Armadillos are placental mammals in the order Cingulata. They form part of the superorder Xenarthra, along with the anteaters and sloths. Twenty-one extant species of armadillo have been described, some of which are distinguished by the number of bands on their armor. All species are native to the Americas, where they inhabit a variety of environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Nine-banded_Armadillo.jpg/330px-Nine-banded_Armadillo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armadillo","wikidata":"Q104840543"},{"title":"Armadillo (character)","blocks":[{"text":"Armadillo (Antonio Rodriguez) is a supervillain appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. Created by writer Mark Gruenwald and artist Paul Neary, he first appeared in Captain America #308 (August 1985). Armadillo is a superhuman with the appearance and abilities of an armadillo, including enhanced strength, durability, and the ability to curl into a ball."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ea/ArmadilloC.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armadillo_(character)","wikidata":"Q1645941"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Pink_Fairy_Armadillo_%28Chlamyphorus_truncatus%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Pink_Fairy_Armadillo_%28Chlamyphorus_truncatus%29_%28cropped%29.jpg